  "articleBody": "Drywall installation in a kitchen demands precision because your walls must support heavy cabinets, conceal complex utilities, and endure humidity. Small shortcuts during the hanging and taping phases often create visible flaws once cabinets and tile are installed.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\n- Closing walls before utility testing: Sealing sheetrock before plumbing lines and electrical rough-ins are tested leads to cutting open fresh drywall to fix leaks or faulty wiring.\n- Using standard board near moisture sources: Standard drywall absorbs moisture behind sinks and dishwashers; using moisture-resistant board protects the wall assembly from indirect dampness.\n- Skipping wood blocking for upper cabinets: Relying solely on drywall anchors or guessing stud centers risks cabinet sag; install solid 2x6 horizontal backing between studs before closing the wall.\n- Placing board seams directly on window and door corners: Aligning seams with door or window edges leads to recurring stress cracks as the house naturally settles.\n- Rushing compound drying times: Applying top coats before joint compound dries fully traps dampness, resulting in shrinkage cracks, bubbling tape, and uneven paint absorption.\n\nWhat to Look for Before Starting\nVerify framing flatness and ensure all mechanical lines sit behind the stud face with protective nail plates in place. If your project involves moving plumbing, rerouting wiring, or prepping for tile, Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC can handle your kitchen finish work. Call 303-505-0866 to schedule an on-site estimate.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
